Chapter: X509
  cites Encode chapter Asn1
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 An X.509 certificate, as RFC 5280 defines it and RFC 8410 extends it to
 the Edwards curves. This chapter PARSES. It does not decide whether a
 certificate is trustworthy -- that is a chain walk, and it is the next
 chapter. Parsing and trusting are separate jobs, and a chapter that does
 both tends to do the second one by accident.

 The one invariant that matters more than any other here: the
 tbsCertificate is kept as the ORIGINAL BYTES, sliced out of the input by
 asn1-raw. It is never decoded and re-encoded. A signature is over a byte
 string, and a re-encoded tbsCertificate is a DIFFERENT byte string -- so a
 parser that rebuilds it is a parser that verifies a signature over
 something nobody ever signed. Every field below is a view onto the input;
 xc-tbs is the input.

 Names are kept as raw DER for the same family of reason. Two names that
 decode to the same string can be different byte strings, and comparing the
 decoded forms is how an implementation ends up believing that one issuer
 is another. The chain walk compares issuer bytes to subject bytes.

 We say:

Section: Object Identifiers

 Compared as encoded bytes, never as decoded numbers -- see the Asn1
 chapter note. These are the CONTENT octets, without the tag and length.

 Ed25519, 1.3.101.112 (RFC 8410).

  x509-oid-ed25519 : List Integer = [43, 101, 112]

 X25519, 1.3.101.110. A key agreement key -- it can appear as a subject
 public key, and it can NEVER verify a signature. The distinction is kept
 in the type so that nothing downstream can confuse the two.

  x509-oid-x25519 : List Integer = [43, 101, 110]

 rsaEncryption, 1.2.840.113549.1.1.1, and sha256WithRSAEncryption,
 1.2.840.113549.1.1.11. Every certificate on Google's chain is signed
 with the second one, which is why they are here.

  x509-oid-rsa : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 134, 247, 13, 1, 1, 1]
  x509-oid-sha256-rsa : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 134, 247, 13, 1, 1, 11]

 sha384WithRSAEncryption, 1.2.840.113549.1.1.12, and
 sha1WithRSAEncryption, 1.2.840.113549.1.1.5.

 SHA-1 is here so that certificates signed with it can be READ, and for
 no other reason. Two of the five roots Codex ships are from 2006 and are
 self-signed with SHA-1; refusing to parse them would mean refusing to
 hold them as anchors, even though an anchor's own signature is never
 checked by anybody. The chain walk answers False for a SHA-1 signature
 unconditionally -- collisions have been practical since 2017 -- so
 reading one and trusting one remain different things.

  x509-oid-sha384-rsa : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 134, 247, 13, 1, 1, 12]
  x509-oid-sha1-rsa : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 134, 247, 13, 1, 1, 5]

 id-ecPublicKey, 1.2.840.10045.2.1. An elliptic-curve key names its curve
 in the AlgorithmIdentifier's parameters, and the curve is the whole
 question: the arithmetic that checks a P-256 signature is not the
 arithmetic that checks a P-384 one, and handing the second to the first
 does not fail loudly -- it computes in the wrong field and answers
 something. So the curve is read, and it decides which of the two
 elliptic-curve key kinds this is.

  x509-oid-ec-public-key : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 206, 61, 2, 1]

 prime256v1, 1.2.840.10045.3.1.7 -- the curve NIST calls P-256 and SEC
 calls secp256r1. It was the only curve this tree could verify on until
 2026-07-28; secp384r1 below is the second. Every other named curve still
 parses into a key that says so about itself.

  x509-oid-p256 : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 206, 61, 3, 1, 7]

 secp384r1, 1.3.132.0.34. The two arcs are encoded differently and that is
 the whole content of the constant: 132 needs two base-128 bytes and 34
 does not.

  x509-oid-p384 : List Integer = [43, 129, 4, 0, 34]

 ecdsa-with-SHA256, 1.2.840.10045.4.3.2, and ecdsa-with-SHA384,
 1.2.840.10045.4.3.3. The Sectigo and Let's Encrypt elliptic-curve
 hierarchies sign their intermediates with the second one, which is why
 both are here. **Both are verified now**, as of 2026-07-28; this note
 said only the first was, and that a P-256 key signing under SHA-384 was
 read but never trusted. The digest half is written and the chain walk
 routes all four combinations of curve and digest.

  x509-oid-ecdsa-sha256 : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 206, 61, 4, 3, 2]
  x509-oid-ecdsa-sha384 : List Integer = [42, 134, 72, 206, 61, 4, 3, 3]

 basicConstraints, 2.5.29.19.

  x509-oid-basic-constraints : List Integer = [85, 29, 19]

Section: Key

 A subject public key, with its algorithm. The algorithm is an enumeration
 and not a byte string, because the whole point is that a caller cannot
 hand an X25519 key to a signature check.

  X509KeyAlg =
    | KeyEd25519
    | KeyX25519
    | KeyRsa
    | KeyEcdsaP256
    | KeyEcdsaP384
    | KeyEcdsaOther
    | KeyUnknown

  X509SigAlg =
    | SigEd25519
    | SigRsaSha256
    | SigRsaSha384
    | SigRsaSha1
    | SigEcdsaSha256
    | SigEcdsaSha384
    | SigUnknown

  X509Key = record {
    xk-alg : X509KeyAlg,
    xk-bytes : List Integer,
    xk-mod : List Integer,
    xk-exp : List Integer
  }

 SubjectPublicKeyInfo ::= SEQUENCE { algorithm AlgorithmIdentifier,
 subjectPublicKey BIT STRING }. For both Edwards OIDs, RFC 8410 says the
 algorithm parameters MUST be absent -- so a SEQUENCE with a second
 element is not a thing we accept.

  x509-spki : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki (buf) (off) =
    when asn1-read-tag buf off asn1-sequence
      is None -> None
      is Just (t) -> x509-spki-parts buf (asn1-children buf t)

  x509-spki-parts : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki-parts (buf) (kids) =
    if list-length kids /= 2 then None
    else x509-spki-alg buf (list-at kids 0) (list-at kids 1)

  x509-spki-alg : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki-alg (buf) (alg) (key) =
    if alg.tlv-tag /= asn1-sequence then None
    else x509-spki-oid buf (asn1-children buf alg) key

 Parameters absent means the AlgorithmIdentifier has exactly one child.

 An AlgorithmIdentifier is an OID and OPTIONAL parameters. The Edwards
 OIDs carry none, and RFC 8410 section 3 says they must not; RSA carries
 an explicit NULL, and RFC 4055 says it must. So one child or two, and
 the second has to be that NULL rather than anything a sender fancies.
 Requiring exactly one child is what made every RSA key in the world
 unparseable here.

  x509-spki-oid : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki-oid (buf) (algkids) (key) =
    if list-length algkids < 1 then None
    else x509-spki-shape buf algkids (x509-alg-of buf algkids) key

  x509-spki-shape : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, X509KeyAlg, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki-shape (buf) (algkids) (alg) (key) =
    if x509-key-params-ok buf algkids alg == False then None
    else x509-spki-bits buf alg key

 The parameter, when present, is NULL for RSA and a named-curve OID for
 an elliptic-curve key. The elliptic-curve case has already been settled
 by x509-curve-of, which insists on the OID rather than tolerating it;
 everything else carries either nothing (RFC 8410 section 3, the Edwards
 curves) or an explicit NULL (RFC 4055, RSA). A parameter of any other
 shape is a certificate this chapter does not understand, and guessing is
 what a parser must never do.

 Every elliptic-curve variant belongs on the True side here, because an EC
 key's parameter IS a named-curve OID and `x509-alg-params-ok` below admits
 only absent-or-NULL. This arm is written out per variant rather than as a
 catch-all, and the reason is worth the line: when `KeyEcdsaP384` was added
 on 2026-07-28 it fell into `is otherwise`, `x509-alg-params-ok` refused the
 curve OID, and every P-384 CERTIFICATE STOPPED PARSING -- not just its key.
 The exhaustiveness checker caught the same omission in a test that listed
 its variants (CDX2070) and could not catch it here, because a catch-all arm
 is exhaustive by construction. **A new variant is silently absorbed by
 every `is otherwise` in the tree.**

  x509-key-params-ok : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, X509KeyAlg -> Boolean
  x509-key-params-ok (buf) (kids) (alg) =
    when alg
      is KeyEcdsaP256 -> True
      is KeyEcdsaP384 -> True
      is KeyEcdsaOther -> True
      is otherwise -> x509-alg-params-ok buf kids

  x509-alg-params-ok : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-alg-params-ok (buf) (kids) =
    if list-length kids == 1 then True
    else if list-length kids /= 2 then False
    else (list-at kids 1).tlv-tag == asn1-null

  x509-alg-of : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> X509KeyAlg
  x509-alg-of (buf) (kids) =
    if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-ed25519 then KeyEd25519
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-x25519 then KeyX25519
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-rsa then KeyRsa
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-ec-public-key then x509-curve-of buf kids
    else KeyUnknown

 RFC 5480 section 2.1.1: for id-ecPublicKey the parameters are NOT
 optional. A key that omits them names no curve, and a key that names one
 we cannot compute on is a key we say so about rather than one we hope
 about.

  x509-curve-of : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> X509KeyAlg
  x509-curve-of (buf) (kids) =
    if list-length kids /= 2 then KeyUnknown
    else if (list-at kids 1).tlv-tag /= asn1-oid then KeyUnknown
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 1) x509-oid-p256 then KeyEcdsaP256
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 1) x509-oid-p384 then KeyEcdsaP384
    else KeyEcdsaOther

  x509-spki-bits : List Integer, X509KeyAlg, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-spki-bits (buf) (alg) (key) =
    when asn1-bit-string buf (key.tlv-hdr)
      is None -> None
      is Just (bs) -> x509-mk-key alg bs

 Both Edwards keys are 32 bytes. A key of any other length is not one of
 these keys, whatever the OID claims. An RSA key is not a byte string at
 all: it is RSAPublicKey ::= SEQUENCE { modulus INTEGER, publicExponent
 INTEGER }, carried inside the BIT STRING, so it is parsed rather than
 measured.

  x509-mk-key : X509KeyAlg, List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-mk-key (alg) (bs) =
    when alg
      is KeyUnknown -> None
      is KeyRsa -> x509-rsa-key bs
      is KeyEcdsaP256 -> x509-p256-key bs
      is KeyEcdsaP384 -> x509-p384-key bs
      is KeyEcdsaOther -> x509-ec-other-key bs
      is otherwise -> x509-mk-edwards alg bs

 A P-256 public key is an uncompressed SEC1 point: the byte 0x04 and then
 two 32-byte coordinates, sixty-five bytes exactly. The compressed forms,
 0x02 and 0x03, are legal SEC1 and are refused: decompressing one is a
 square root in the field, which is arithmetic we would be performing on
 an attacker's behalf before anything has been decided to be trustworthy.
 Whether the point lies ON the curve is not asked here -- that is curve
 arithmetic and it belongs with the curve arithmetic, in
 ecdsa-p256-verify, which asks it before it does anything else.

  x509-p256-key : List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-p256-key (bs) =
    if list-length bs /= 65 then None
    else if list-at bs 0 /= 4 then None
    else Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyEcdsaP256, xk-bytes = bs, xk-mod = [], xk-exp = [] })

 A P-384 point is ninety-seven bytes on the same reasoning, and the length
 is checked against the CURVE the OID named rather than against whichever
 length the byte string happens to have. A sixty-five byte point under the
 P-384 OID is not a short P-384 key, it is a P-256 key wearing the wrong
 label, and the two are told apart here rather than in the arithmetic.

  x509-p384-key : List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-p384-key (bs) =
    if list-length bs /= 97 then None
    else if list-at bs 0 /= 4 then None
    else Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyEcdsaP384, xk-bytes = bs, xk-mod = [], xk-exp = [] })

 A key on any other named curve is kept whole and unexamined, because
 nothing here can do anything with it and pretending otherwise would be
 the whole problem in miniature. It parses so that the certificates
 carrying it can be read -- most elliptic-curve roots are P-384 -- and
 x509-can-verify answers False for it, so it can never sit in an issuer
 position.

  x509-ec-other-key : List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-ec-other-key (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 33 then None
    else Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyEcdsaOther, xk-bytes = bs, xk-mod = [], xk-exp = [] })

  x509-mk-edwards : X509KeyAlg, List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-mk-edwards (alg) (bs) =
    if list-length bs /= 32 then None
    else Just (X509Key { xk-alg = alg, xk-bytes = bs, xk-mod = [], xk-exp = [] })

  x509-rsa-key : List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-rsa-key (bs) =
    when asn1-read-tag bs 0 asn1-sequence
      is None -> None
      is Just (s) -> x509-rsa-parts bs (asn1-children bs s)

  x509-rsa-parts : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-rsa-parts (bs) (kids) =
    if list-length kids /= 2 then None
    else if (list-at kids 0).tlv-tag /= asn1-integer then None
    else if (list-at kids 1).tlv-tag /= asn1-integer then None
    else x509-rsa-mk (x509-unsign (asn1-content bs (list-at kids 0))) (x509-unsign (asn1-content bs (list-at kids 1)))

  x509-rsa-mk : List Integer, List Integer -> Maybe X509Key
  x509-rsa-mk (m) (e) =
    if list-length m < 64 then None
    else if list-length e < 1 then None
    else Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyRsa, xk-bytes = [], xk-mod = m, xk-exp = e })

 A DER INTEGER is signed, so a modulus whose top bit is set carries a
 leading zero byte to keep it positive. That byte is part of the encoding
 and not part of the number, and leaving it on makes the modulus 257
 bytes wide -- which then does not match the 256-byte signature it is
 supposed to verify, and the failure looks like a bad signature rather
 than a bad parse.

  x509-unsign : List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-unsign (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 2 then bs
    else if list-at bs 0 /= 0 then bs
    else x509-drop-first bs

  x509-drop-first : List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-drop-first (bs) = x509-drop-loop bs 1 []

  x509-drop-loop : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-drop-loop (bs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length bs then acc
    else x509-drop-loop bs (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

  x509-can-verify : X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-can-verify (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyEd25519 -> True
      is KeyRsa -> True
      is KeyEcdsaP256 -> True
      is KeyEcdsaP384 -> True
      is otherwise -> False

Section: The Certificate

 Certificate ::= SEQUENCE { tbsCertificate, signatureAlgorithm,
 signatureValue }. xc-tbs is the raw signed bytes; everything else is a
 view onto them.

  X509Cert = record {
    xc-tbs : List Integer,
    xc-sig : List Integer,
    xc-sig-alg : X509SigAlg,
    xc-version : Integer,
    xc-serial : List Integer,
    xc-issuer : List Integer,
    xc-subject : List Integer,
    xc-not-before : List Integer,
    xc-not-after : List Integer,
    xc-key : X509Key,
    xc-is-ca : Boolean,
    xc-dns-names : List (List Integer),
    xc-ip-addrs : List (List Integer)
  }

  x509-parse : List Integer -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-parse (buf) =
    when asn1-read-tag buf 0 asn1-sequence
      is None -> None
      is Just (t) -> x509-outer buf (asn1-children buf t)

 The outer SEQUENCE has exactly three elements. A certificate with a
 fourth is not a certificate we understand, and we do not guess.

  x509-outer : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-outer (buf) (kids) =
    if list-length kids /= 3 then None
    else x509-outer-sig buf (list-at kids 0) (list-at kids 1) (list-at kids 2)

  x509-outer-sig : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Asn1Tlv, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-outer-sig (buf) (tbs) (alg) (sig) =
    when asn1-bit-string buf (sig.tlv-hdr)
      is None -> None
      is Just (sb) -> x509-tbs buf tbs (x509-sig-alg-of buf alg) sb

 An Ed25519 signature is 64 bytes, always, and one of any other length is
 refused here rather than handed to the verifier to puzzle over. An RSA
 signature is as wide as the modulus, which this certificate does not
 carry -- the ISSUER's does -- so its width is checked where the issuer's
 key is known, in the chain walk, and not here.

 An ECDSA signature has no fixed width at all: it is DER, SEQUENCE of two
 INTEGERs, and both are variable because a leading zero appears exactly
 when the top bit would otherwise make the value negative. Eight bytes is
 the smallest structure that could possibly be one. The real check is not
 a width -- it is the decode, in x509-ecdsa-rs, and a signature that does
 not decode verifies nothing.

 A signature algorithm we do not recognise refuses the whole certificate.
 That is deliberate and it is the conservative direction: an unparsed
 algorithm must never reach a verifier that would then pick one.

  x509-tbs : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, X509SigAlg, List Integer -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-tbs (buf) (tbs) (salg) (sig) =
    when salg
      is SigUnknown -> None
      is SigEd25519 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig == 64)
      is SigRsaSha256 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig >= 64)
      is SigRsaSha384 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig >= 64)
      is SigRsaSha1 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig >= 64)
      is SigEcdsaSha256 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig >= 8)
      is SigEcdsaSha384 -> x509-tbs-sized buf tbs salg sig (list-length sig >= 8)

  x509-tbs-sized : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, X509SigAlg, List Integer, Boolean -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-tbs-sized (buf) (tbs) (salg) (sig) (ok) =
    if ok == False then None
    else if tbs.tlv-tag /= asn1-sequence then None
    else x509-fields buf tbs sig salg (asn1-children buf tbs)

  x509-sig-alg-of : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> X509SigAlg
  x509-sig-alg-of (buf) (alg) =
    if alg.tlv-tag /= asn1-sequence then SigUnknown
    else x509-sig-alg-kids buf (asn1-children buf alg)

  x509-sig-alg-kids : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> X509SigAlg
  x509-sig-alg-kids (buf) (kids) =
    if x509-alg-params-ok buf kids == False then SigUnknown
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-ed25519 then SigEd25519
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-sha256-rsa then SigRsaSha256
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-sha384-rsa then SigRsaSha384
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-sha1-rsa then SigRsaSha1
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-ecdsa-sha256 then SigEcdsaSha256
    else if asn1-oid-is buf (list-at kids 0) x509-oid-ecdsa-sha384 then SigEcdsaSha384
    else SigUnknown

Section: The TBSCertificate

 TBSCertificate ::= SEQUENCE { version [0] EXPLICIT DEFAULT v1,
 serialNumber, signature, issuer, validity, subject,
 subjectPublicKeyInfo, ... , extensions [3] EXPLICIT OPTIONAL }.

 The version is optional and defaults to v1. We require v3, because v1 has
 no extensions -- so it has no basicConstraints, so nothing in it can say
 whether it is a CA, and a chain walk over v1 certificates is a chain walk
 that cannot tell a leaf from an authority.

  x509-fields : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-fields (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) =
    if list-length kids < 7 then None
    else if (list-at kids 0).tlv-tag /= asn1-ctx0 then None
    else x509-version buf tbs sig salg kids

  x509-version : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-version (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) =
    when asn1-small-int buf ((list-at kids 0).tlv-off)
      is None -> None
      is Just (v) -> if v /= 2 then None else x509-body buf tbs sig salg kids v

 With the version present the field order is fixed: serial, signature,
 issuer, validity, subject, spki. The inner signature AlgorithmIdentifier
 must match the outer one -- RFC 5280 says so, and a certificate whose two
 algorithm fields disagree is one where the signed statement and the thing
 being checked are not the same statement.

  x509-body : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-body (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) (v) =
    if x509-sig-alg-eq (x509-sig-alg-of buf (list-at kids 2)) salg == False then None
    else x509-serial buf tbs sig salg kids v

  x509-sig-alg-eq : X509SigAlg, X509SigAlg -> Boolean
  x509-sig-alg-eq (a) (b) = x509-sig-alg-code a == x509-sig-alg-code b

  x509-sig-alg-code : X509SigAlg -> Integer
  x509-sig-alg-code (a) =
    when a
      is SigEd25519 -> 1
      is SigRsaSha256 -> 2
      is SigRsaSha384 -> 3
      is SigRsaSha1 -> 4
      is SigEcdsaSha256 -> 5
      is SigEcdsaSha384 -> 6
      is SigUnknown -> 0

  x509-serial : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-serial (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) (v) =
    when asn1-int-bytes buf ((list-at kids 1).tlv-hdr)
      is None -> None
      is Just (sn) -> x509-validity buf tbs sig salg kids v sn

 Validity ::= SEQUENCE { notBefore Time, notAfter Time }. The times are
 kept as their raw bytes. This chapter has no clock and does not want one:
 comparing certificate times is the chain walk's job, and it takes the
 current time from its caller.

  x509-validity : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, List Integer -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-validity (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) (v) (sn) =
    when x509-times buf (list-at kids 4)
      is None -> None
      is Just (tt) -> x509-key-of buf tbs sig salg kids v sn tt

  X509Times = record {
    xt-not-before : List Integer,
    xt-not-after : List Integer
  }

  x509-times : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Times
  x509-times (buf) (val) =
    if val.tlv-tag /= asn1-sequence then None
    else x509-times-of buf (asn1-children buf val)

  x509-times-of : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe X509Times
  x509-times-of (buf) (kids) =
    if list-length kids /= 2 then None
    else Just (X509Times { xt-not-before = asn1-content buf (list-at kids 0),
                           xt-not-after = asn1-content buf (list-at kids 1) })

  x509-key-of : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, List Integer, X509Times -> Maybe X509Cert
  x509-key-of (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) (v) (sn) (tt) =
    when x509-spki buf ((list-at kids 6).tlv-hdr)
      is None -> None
      is Just (k) -> Just (x509-mk buf tbs sig salg kids v sn tt k)

  x509-mk : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, List Integer, X509SigAlg, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, List Integer, X509Times, X509Key -> X509Cert
  x509-mk (buf) (tbs) (sig) (salg) (kids) (v) (sn) (tt) (k) =
    X509Cert { xc-tbs = asn1-raw buf tbs,
               xc-sig = sig,
               xc-sig-alg = salg,
               xc-version = v,
               xc-serial = sn,
               xc-issuer = asn1-raw buf (list-at kids 3),
               xc-subject = asn1-raw buf (list-at kids 5),
               xc-not-before = tt.xt-not-before,
               xc-not-after = tt.xt-not-after,
               xc-key = k,
               xc-is-ca = x509-ca-flag buf kids,
               xc-dns-names = x509-alt-names buf kids x509-gn-dns,
               xc-ip-addrs = x509-alt-names buf kids x509-gn-ip }

Section: subjectAltName

 The names a certificate actually asserts. Until 2026-07-26 nothing here
 read them, and nothing anywhere asked whether a certificate belonged to
 the host that was dialled -- so a leaf issued by a trusted authority
 authenticated for every host in the world. That is the hole this section
 exists to close, and the check that consumes it is x509-identity-matches
 in X509Chain.

 subjectAltName is OID 2.5.29.17 and its extnValue wraps GeneralNames ::=
 SEQUENCE OF GeneralName. GeneralName is a CHOICE of context-tagged
 alternatives; the two that name a network peer are dNSName [2], an
 IA5String, and iPAddress [7], four bytes for IPv4. Both are PRIMITIVE
 context tags, so the tag byte is #82 and #87 rather than the constructed
 #A2 and #A7 -- reading them as constructed finds no children and answers
 that a certificate carrying names carries none.

 The commonName is deliberately NOT read. RFC 6125 deprecated it as an
 identity in 2011 and every browser stopped honouring it years ago; a
 certificate that asserts no subjectAltName asserts no identity, and the
 honest answer to "does this name my peer" is then No. Accepting the CN as
 a fallback would mean the old fixtures kept passing, which is exactly the
 wrong reason to accept anything.

  x509-oid-subject-alt-name : List Integer = [85, 29, 17]

  x509-gn-dns : Integer = #82
  x509-gn-ip : Integer = #87

 The walk mirrors x509-ca-flag's: the extensions live in the [3] EXPLICIT
 member of the TBS, wrapped in a SEQUENCE, and each extension is a
 SEQUENCE of OID, optional critical flag, and an OCTET STRING holding the
 real DER.

  x509-alt-names : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-alt-names (buf) (kids) (want) = x509-find-san buf kids 7 want

  x509-find-san : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-find-san (buf) (kids) (i) (want) =
    if i >= list-length kids then []
    else if (list-at kids i).tlv-tag == asn1-ctx3 then x509-san-exts buf (list-at kids i) want
    else x509-find-san buf kids (i + 1) want

  x509-san-exts : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-san-exts (buf) (ctx) (want) =
    when asn1-nth (asn1-children buf ctx) 0
      is None -> []
      is Just (seq) -> x509-scan-san buf (asn1-children buf seq) 0 want

  x509-scan-san : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-scan-san (buf) (exts) (i) (want) =
    if i >= list-length exts then []
    else if x509-ext-is-san buf (list-at exts i) then x509-san-value buf (list-at exts i) want
    else x509-scan-san buf exts (i + 1) want

  x509-ext-is-san : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-ext-is-san (buf) (e) =
    when asn1-nth (asn1-children buf e) 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (o) -> asn1-oid-is buf o x509-oid-subject-alt-name

  x509-san-value : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-san-value (buf) (e) (want) =
    when asn1-last (asn1-children buf e)
      is None -> []
      is Just (v) -> x509-san-inner buf v want

  x509-san-inner : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-san-inner (buf) (v) (want) =
    if v.tlv-tag /= asn1-octet-string then []
    else x509-san-seq buf (asn1-read-tag buf (v.tlv-off) asn1-sequence) want

  x509-san-seq : List Integer, Maybe Asn1Tlv, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  x509-san-seq (buf) (m) (want) =
    when m
      is None -> []
      is Just (s) -> x509-san-collect buf (asn1-children buf s) 0 want []

  x509-san-collect : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> List (List Integer)
  x509-san-collect (buf) (gns) (i) (want) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length gns then acc
    else if (list-at gns i).tlv-tag /= want then x509-san-collect buf gns (i + 1) want acc
    else x509-san-collect buf gns (i + 1) want (list-push acc (asn1-content buf (list-at gns i)))

Section: basicConstraints

 Extensions ::= [3] EXPLICIT SEQUENCE OF Extension, and Extension ::=
 SEQUENCE { extnID OID, critical BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE, extnValue OCTET
 STRING }. We are after one bit: cA.

 cA DEFAULTS TO FALSE, and the default is not written down -- so a
 basicConstraints extension with an empty SEQUENCE means "not a CA", and
 an absent extension means the same. Defaulting the other way, or treating
 "I could not parse it" as True, is how a leaf certificate becomes an
 authority that can sign anything. The absence of evidence is evidence of
 absence here, deliberately.

  x509-ca-flag : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-ca-flag (buf) (kids) = x509-find-exts buf kids 7

  x509-find-exts : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-find-exts (buf) (kids) (i) =
    if i >= list-length kids then False
    else if (list-at kids i).tlv-tag == asn1-ctx3 then x509-exts-seq buf (list-at kids i)
    else x509-find-exts buf kids (i + 1)

  x509-exts-seq : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-exts-seq (buf) (ctx) =
    when asn1-nth (asn1-children buf ctx) 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (seq) -> x509-scan-exts buf (asn1-children buf seq) 0

  x509-scan-exts : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-scan-exts (buf) (exts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length exts then False
    else if x509-ext-is-bc buf (list-at exts i) then x509-bc-value buf (list-at exts i)
    else x509-scan-exts buf exts (i + 1)

  x509-ext-is-bc : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-ext-is-bc (buf) (e) =
    when asn1-nth (asn1-children buf e) 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (o) -> asn1-oid-is buf o x509-oid-basic-constraints

 The extnValue is an OCTET STRING wrapping the real DER. The cA boolean is
 the first element of the SEQUENCE inside it, if there is one.

  x509-bc-value : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-bc-value (buf) (e) =
    when asn1-last (asn1-children buf e)
      is None -> False
      is Just (v) -> x509-bc-inner buf v

  x509-bc-inner : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-bc-inner (buf) (v) =
    if v.tlv-tag /= asn1-octet-string then False
    else x509-bc-seq buf (asn1-read-tag buf (v.tlv-off) asn1-sequence)

  x509-bc-seq : List Integer, Maybe Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-bc-seq (buf) (m) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (s) -> x509-bc-bool buf (asn1-children buf s)

 DER says a BOOLEAN is TRUE when its byte is anything other than zero, but
 it also says TRUE must be encoded as 0xFF. We accept only 0xFF: a
 certificate carrying 0x01 here is not DER, and the one thing we will not
 do is be lenient about the bit that decides who may sign.

  x509-bc-bool : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-bc-bool (buf) (kids) =
    when asn1-nth kids 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (b) -> x509-bool-true buf b

  x509-bool-true : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Boolean
  x509-bool-true (buf) (b) =
    if b.tlv-tag /= asn1-boolean then False
    else if b.tlv-len /= 1 then False
    else list-at buf (b.tlv-off) == #FF

  asn1-last : List Asn1Tlv -> Maybe Asn1Tlv
  asn1-last (ts) =
    if list-length ts < 1 then None
    else Just (list-at ts (list-length ts - 1))

Section: An ECDSA Signature As DER

 A certificate does not carry the fixed sixty-four bytes that TLS and the
 JOSE family use for a P-256 signature. It carries DER: SEQUENCE { INTEGER
 r, INTEGER s }, and both integers are variable width because a leading
 zero appears exactly when the top bit would otherwise make the value
 negative. So the two forms are not the same bytes, and this is where one
 becomes the other.

 It is a decode and not a reshuffle. Every rule the Asn1 chapter enforces
 about a DER INTEGER applies here, and the reason it matters is that a
 signature is the one field an attacker chooses freely: a decoder that
 accepts several spellings of one signature has made a signature into a
 value with several names.

  x509-ecdsa-rs : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-rs (sig) (w) =
    when asn1-read-tag sig 0 asn1-sequence
      is None -> None
      is Just (t) -> x509-ecdsa-whole sig t w

 The SEQUENCE must be the WHOLE signature. Trailing bytes are bytes nobody
 signed and nobody accounted for, and ignoring them is how one signature
 acquires unboundedly many encodings.

  x509-ecdsa-whole : List Integer, Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-whole (sig) (t) (w) =
    if asn1-end t /= list-length sig then None
    else x509-ecdsa-parts sig (asn1-children sig t) w

  x509-ecdsa-parts : List Integer, List Asn1Tlv, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-parts (sig) (kids) (w) =
    if list-length kids /= 2 then None
    else x509-ecdsa-two (x509-ecdsa-int sig (list-at kids 0)) (x509-ecdsa-int sig (list-at kids 1)) w

  x509-ecdsa-two : Maybe (List Integer), Maybe (List Integer), Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-two (mr) (ms) (w) =
    when mr
      is None -> None
      is Just (r) -> x509-ecdsa-join r ms w

  x509-ecdsa-join : List Integer, Maybe (List Integer), Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-join (r) (ms) (w) =
    when ms
      is None -> None
      is Just (s) -> x509-ecdsa-fit r s w

 One INTEGER, as thirty-two big-endian bytes. asn1-int-bytes has already
 refused a non-minimal encoding, so a leading zero here is present for
 exactly one reason -- to keep a value whose top bit is set positive --
 and dropping it is safe. A top byte with the sign bit set and no zero in
 front of it is a NEGATIVE integer, which no ECDSA component ever is, and
 a value still wider than thirty-two bytes afterwards is not a scalar on
 this curve.

  x509-ecdsa-int : List Integer, Asn1Tlv -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-int (sig) (t) =
    if t.tlv-tag /= asn1-integer then None
    else x509-ecdsa-bytes (asn1-int-bytes sig (t.tlv-hdr))

  x509-ecdsa-bytes : Maybe (List Integer) -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-bytes (m) =
    when m
      is None -> None
      is Just (bs) -> x509-ecdsa-positive bs

  x509-ecdsa-positive : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-positive (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 1 then None
    else if bit-and (list-at bs 0) #80 == #80 then None
    else Just (x509-unsign bs)

 Left-padded to the width the verifier takes. A short r or s is not
 malformed: a scalar whose top byte happens to be zero is encoded in
 thirty-one bytes, which occurs about once in every two hundred and
 fifty-six signatures. Refusing those, or truncating instead of padding,
 is a verifier that rejects a valid signature at a rate low enough to
 reach production and high enough to be seen there.

  x509-ecdsa-fit : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ecdsa-fit (r) (s) (w) =
    if list-length r > w then None
    else if list-length s > w then None
    else Just (x509-zeros (w - list-length r) [] & r & x509-zeros (w - list-length s) [] & s)

  x509-zeros : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-zeros (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else x509-zeros (n - 1) (list-push acc 0)
